#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""SYRD-436: the current generated-layout builders, against the launcher they came out of.

The layout a new project is generated with -- `_new_project_layout_payload`,
from its leaves (`_new_project_layout_leaves`), a single row
(`_single_row_layout_payload`) through `NEW_PROJECT_SINGLE_ROW_LAYOUT_MAX_ROLES`
roles, and above that a row-major grid (`_row_major_grid_layout_payload`) of at
most `NEW_PROJECT_GRID_PANES_PER_ROW` panes a row -- moved unchanged into
`scripts/project_layouts.py`; the launcher re-exports all six. This pins what
makes that safe:

- **No cycle, one set of objects**, whichever module is imported first; the
  module alone loads no other Switchyard module, never the launcher; none of the
  builders has a definition-time default; the thresholds are plain literals.
- **Seams (rule 24):** the builders read each other and both thresholds through
  the launcher as often as before, so a patch there reaches them: every case
  below records them there, and rebinds each threshold and helper there to see
  the effect.
- **Readers:** six production modules read the builders through the launcher;
  `legacy_layouts`' list of known layouts is driven and must start with the
  current builder's answer, read there.
- **The behaviour is the baseline's:** the layout for role counts either side
  of the row/grid threshold and well past it, the leaves, the single row and the
  grid alone, recorded as values, as the exact JSON text (key order included)
  and by container type. `GOLDEN` below was produced by running the BASELINE
  launcher's own definitions over the very cases embedded here (`gold436.py`),
  not typed; it is byte-identical under `env -i`, in a normal role pane and
  with another HOME, USER and COLUMNS.

Nothing touches a live layout. Spawns, every exec, signals, account and group
lookups and socket connections are refused for each case.
"""

